---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureToSheet from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureToSheet.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-csv">
  <PdfSecureToSheet client:load format="csv" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Why take the CSV rather than the workbook</h2>
      <p>
        CSV is the format you want when a machine is the next reader. It is plain text, so it opens
        in anything, diffs cleanly in Git, streams into a database loader, and cannot carry a macro.
        An .xlsx is a zip of XML with types, styles and formulas: useful for a person, extra work for a
        script.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          For pandas, R or a database, use CSV. Run <code>pd.read_csv("statement.csv")</code> and you
          are done.
        </li>
        <li>
          To commit extracted data to a repository, use CSV. The diff is readable.
        </li>
        <li>
          For a colleague to edit, use <a href="/pdf-to-excel">PDF to Excel</a>, so each page keeps its
          own sheet.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The escaping rules used</h2>
      <p>
        Output follows RFC 4180, which is what Excel, Numbers, LibreOffice, Python's
        <code>csv</code> module and every serious parser expect:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Fields are separated by a comma and rows by a newline.</li>
        <li>
          A field containing a comma, a double quote or a line break is wrapped in double quotes.
        </li>
        <li>A double quote inside a quoted field is written twice.</li>
        <li>Nothing else is escaped: no backslashes, no HTML entities.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So a cell reading <code>Smith, John "Jack"</code> is written as
        <code>"Smith, John ""Jack"""</code>. This is the part hand-rolled exporters get wrong, and
        it is exactly the part that silently corrupts a column of names.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How multi-page documents are laid out</h2>
      <p>
        Each page's rows are written in order, with a blank line between pages. That gives you one
        file for a whole statement with an unambiguous boundary. A blank line is never valid inside a
        record, so it is safe to split on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your pages repeat a header row, you will get that header once per page. Filtering those
        out is a one-liner in whatever you load the file into, and leaving them in is the honest
        thing for the exporter to do: guessing which rows are headers is how data quietly goes
        missing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Common sources and what to expect</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Source document</th>
              <th scope="col">Typical result</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Bank statement downloaded from online banking</td>
              <td>Very good. Fixed columns, consistent alignment, digitally generated</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Invoice from accounting software</td>
              <td>Good for the line-item table; header blocks come through as stray rows</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Report exported from a database tool</td>
              <td>Excellent. These are the cleanest tables</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scientific paper with a results table</td>
              <td>Mixed. Multi-level headers and footnote markers confuse the columns</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Scanned or photographed statement</td>
              <td>Nothing. No text layer exists to read</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Government form with boxed fields</td>
              <td>Poor. Boxes are not columns, and the field labels mix with the values</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Numbers, dates and locales</h2>
      <p>
        Cells are exported exactly as they appear in the document: as text, not as parsed values.
        That is deliberate: <code>1.240,00</code> means one thousand two hundred and forty in Berlin
        and something else entirely in London, and a converter that guesses will eventually guess
        wrong on your money.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do the conversion where you have the context: set the locale when importing into your
        spreadsheet, or parse explicitly in your script. Dates behave the same way:
        <code>03/04/2026</code> stays as written and is not read differently.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Nothing leaves the tab</h2>
      <p>
        Financial documents are the single most common input to this tool, which makes the
        no-upload property more than a nicety. The PDF is decoded by pdf.js inside this page, the
        clustering is ordinary JavaScript, and the CSV string is built in memory and handed to your
        download folder. There is no endpoint to send it to: the site is static files on a CDN with a
        content security policy that allows no third-party connections.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
